# By-value Checker bridges reached from inside a *mut function.
#
# Each line is a `*mut` function that writes `(*c).method(...)` where `method`
# is an `impl Checker` function returning a Checker. That copies the entire
# 172-field Checker onto the stack going in and copies another one back out --
# a TypeEnv, a BorrowEnv, a ~12.5 KB UnitRegistry and a RefinementTable per
# call. The `*mut` transcription exists precisely to avoid that, so every
# entry here is the transcription defeating its own purpose.
#
# THIS IS NOT A STYLE COMPLAINT. On 2026-09-09 the bridge inside
# checker_finish_binary_units_inplace segfaulted the compiler the first time
# control reached it. It had never run before: the guard above it returned
# early while unit_id was always -1, and wiring units through made it live.
# A latent crash sitting behind a dead feature is the shape to watch for.
#
# THE RULE: this list may SHRINK. It may not grow. A new bridge fails
# scripts/ci/checker_spine_parity_gate.sh. Remove a line when the function is
# transcribed properly.
#
# Measured 2026-09-09 against self-hosted/check/check.sio, with line comments
# stripped before scanning -- an earlier version of the gate matched a bridge
# quoted inside a comment that documented its own removal.
#
# A NOTE ON WHAT THIS LEDGER IS FOR, learned the hard way on 2026-09-09.
# Two entries were briefly added here for a NEW bridge, on the reasoning that
# the collect pass is shallow and could afford one whole-Checker copy per
# `ontology` declaration. It could not: the Checker is ~8 MB, copying it onto
# the stack is fatal with no recursion required, and it segfaulted 40 of the
# 42 ontology fixtures. The entries were removed and the bridge deleted.
#
# THE LEDGER IS FOR EXISTING DEBT, NOT A PERMIT FOR NEW DEBT. Recording a
# bridge does not make it survivable, and this gate cannot tell a safe one
# from a fatal one -- it only makes an UNNOTICED addition impossible.
#
# ONE ENTRY IS A KNOWN NEXT TARGET: checker_check_call_arg_unit_boundary_inplace
# bridges to report_unit_call_mismatch. It is the call-argument twin of the
# binary-operator path fixed on 2026-09-09, and it will start executing for
# the first time now that unit_id is real at call boundaries.

checker_check_binary_with_operand_types_inplace->record_hyper_expr
checker_check_binding_init_expr_bridge->check_binding_init_expr
checker_check_call_arg_refinement_boundary_inplace->report_warning_at
checker_check_call_arg_unit_boundary_inplace->report_unit_call_mismatch
checker_check_call_expr_inplace->report_hypothesis_penalty_warning
checker_check_callee_effects_named_inplace->report_effect_error_from
checker_check_expr_mut->check_expr
checker_check_items_inner_return_error_count_mut->check_items
checker_check_items_inner_store_return_error_count_mut->check_items
checker_check_items_inner_store_stop_mut->check_items
checker_check_stmt_bridge->check_stmt
checker_collect_then_check_inner_emit_status_mut->check_items
checker_collect_then_check_inner_emit_status_mut->collect_items
checker_ontology_boundary_check_call_arg_contract_inplace->check_call_arg_ontology_boundary
